This article documents a pathology whole-slide imaging pipeline implemented as a script-first research workflow in Python. The repository defines a complete sequence comprising artifact detection on archived whole-slide images, database-backed patch extraction from heterogeneous annotation formats, human-in-the-loop and graph-based cleaning of cancer patches, packaging of cleaned patches into a source HDF5 dataset, patient-level crossfold generation with optional train-only stain normalization, scientific sanity checks, smart sampling of the training split, repeated learning-rate screening, single-model segmentation training, validation-only ensemble recipe optimization, and frozen test-time inference. The implementation emphasizes explicit file contracts, patient-disjoint splitting, deterministic identifiers, and provenance-aware fail-closed checks across downstream stages. Quantitative performance results are not stated here because no authoritative result artifacts were provided to the repository inspection workflow. Corresponding results, cohort statistics, and final experiment-specific model selections therefore remain work in progress.
